  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why does welding need so much current?",
      "a": "A big current, often 50 to 300 amps at a low voltage, is needed to keep the arc burning and to deliver enough heat to melt thick metal quickly. The welding machine turns mains power into this safe, high current.",
      "asks": [
        "How many amps does a welder use?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a welding electrode?",
      "a": "The electrode carries current to the arc. In stick welding it is a metal rod with a flux coating that melts into the joint; in MIG welding it is a wire fed continuously from a reel; in TIG welding it is tungsten that does not melt.",
      "asks": [
        "What are welding rods made of?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How hot is a welding arc?",
      "a": "A welding arc reaches several thousand degrees Celsius, hotter than the melting point of any common metal. It also gives off intense ultraviolet light that can burn eyes and skin, so welders wear dark masks and covered clothing.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do welders wear dark masks?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a weld join two pieces?",
      "a": "The edges of both pieces melt and flow together with any filler metal into one weld pool. As it freezes, the crystals grow across from one piece into the other, so they become one continuous piece of metal.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a weld as strong as the metal?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do welders use shielding gas?",
      "a": "Air contains oxygen and nitrogen that react with hot molten metal, making the weld brittle and full of holes. A flow of gas like argon or carbon dioxide pushes the air away from the weld pool.",
      "asks": [
        "What does flux do in welding?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Welding arcs give off intense ultraviolet light that can cause a painful eye injury called arc eye.",
    "A typical stick welder works at about 50 to 300 amps."
  ],
